CAT = "cfal1Ethics"; SEG = "ETH"
Q = [
("An analyst combines public filings, store visits and nonmaterial comments from a supplier to conclude that a retailer will miss its earnings forecast. Under the CFA Institute Standards, issuing a sell recommendation is:",
 "Permitted under the mosaic theory", ["A breach of Material Nonpublic Information", "Permitted only after the earnings release", "Allowed only with the issuer's consent"],
 "The mosaic theory lets an analyst reach a conclusion from public information and nonmaterial nonpublic information. None of the pieces is material nonpublic information on its own, so Standard II(A) is not breached."),
("A CFO accidentally tells a portfolio manager about an unannounced merger. The manager's best course of action is to:",
 "Not trade and urge public disclosure", ["Trade only for clients, not himself", "Trade after waiting one business day", "Tell the firm's other analysts first"],
 "Standard II(A) prohibits acting on, or causing others to act on, material nonpublic information. The manager should not trade and should encourage the company to make the information public."),
("A firm changes its rating on a stock from buy to sell. Under Fair Dealing, the change should be communicated to clients:",
 "Fairly, so none are disadvantaged", ["Largest accounts first, by size", "Only to clients who hold the stock", "In alphabetical order by name"],
 "Standard III(B) requires fair, not necessarily identical, treatment. Firms may offer different service levels, but no client may be disadvantaged by when or how a recommendation change reaches them."),
("A portfolio manager wants to buy a stock for her own account on the same day she is buying it for clients. Under Priority of Transactions she should:",
 "Trade after the client orders", ["Trade before the client orders", "Trade at the same time as clients", "Trade only if clients pay a fee"],
 "Standard VI(B) gives client and employer transactions priority over personal transactions. Trading ahead of clients, known as front-running, is the classic breach."),
("An adviser receives a fee from a lawyer for every client he refers. Under the Standards he must:",
 "Disclose it to all parties", ["Refuse it in every case", "Disclose it to the lawyer alone", "Report it to the regulator alone"],
 "Standard VI(C), Referral Fees, requires disclosure of any compensation or benefit for a referral to the employer, clients and prospective clients so they can judge any bias."),
("A member is offered paid consulting work that could compete with her employer's business. Before accepting she needs:",
 "Written consent from her employer", ["Verbal approval from a colleague", "Approval from the client only", "Nothing, if done outside work hours"],
 "Standard IV(B), Additional Compensation Arrangements, requires written consent from all parties involved, which includes the employer, before accepting outside compensation that may conflict with the employer's interest."),
("A client gives her portfolio manager tickets to a sports final as thanks for a strong year. Under Independence and Objectivity the manager should:",
 "Disclose the gift to his employer", ["Return the gift without comment", "Accept it with no disclosure", "Disclose it to the issuer's board"],
 "Gifts from clients based on past performance are less likely to impair independence than gifts from issuers, so Standard I(B) allows them, but the member should disclose them to the employer."),
("A charterholder files for personal bankruptcy after a business failure, with no fraud involved. Under the Misconduct standard this is:",
 "Not by itself a violation", ["A violation in every case", "A breach of Loyalty to employer", "A breach of Diligence"],
 "Standard I(D) covers dishonesty, fraud and deceit, and conduct that reflects on professional integrity. A bankruptcy without fraud does not, on its own, breach the Standards."),
("A member works in a country whose securities law is less strict than the Code and Standards. The member should follow:",
 "The stricter of the two", ["The local law in every case", "The Code only if clients agree", "The law of the client's country"],
 "Under Standard I(A), Knowledge of the Law, a member follows the more strict of applicable law and the Code and Standards. Where law is stricter, the law prevails."),
("While still employed, a portfolio manager plans to start a competing firm. Which action is consistent with Loyalty to the employer?",
 "Registering the new firm's name", ["Copying client files to a home drive", "Soliciting clients before resigning", "Taking the firm's trading models"],
 "Standard IV(A) allows a member to prepare to compete, such as forming the new entity, while still employed. Taking employer records or soliciting clients before leaving is a breach."),
("To win a mandate, a manager shows a prospect returns from only her three best accounts as if they were her typical results. This breaches:",
 "Performance Presentation", ["Fair Dealing", "Communication with Clients", "Record Retention"],
 "Standard III(D) requires performance information to be fair, accurate and complete. Showing selected accounts as representative misleads the prospect about typical results."),
("A member learns that a client is laundering money through his account. Under Preservation of Confidentiality, the member:",
 "May report it to the authorities", ["Must keep it confidential", "Must tell the client first", "Must close the account in silence"],
 "Standard III(E) protects client information, but the duty does not cover illegal activity: a member may need to disclose it to proper authorities, and should follow firm compliance procedures."),
("A client asks the adviser to buy a speculative stock that is unsuitable for her stated objectives. The adviser should:",
 "Discuss it, then may execute it", ["Refuse the order in every case", "Execute it with no comment", "Rewrite the client's IPS on her own"],
 "Under Standard III(C), Suitability, an unsolicited trade outside the client's mandate should be discussed with the client. If it does not change the whole portfolio's suitability, the adviser may carry it out."),
("A research analyst bases a report on a third-party provider's earnings forecasts. Under Diligence and Reasonable Basis she should:",
 "Review the provider's methods", ["Copy the forecasts without review", "Cite the provider and nothing more", "Avoid third-party data entirely"],
 "Standard V(A) allows members to rely on others' research if they make reasonable efforts to judge its quality, such as reviewing the provider's assumptions and methods."),
("A research report says a company's sales will grow 12% next year without labeling it as an estimate. This most likely breaches:",
 "Communication with Clients", ["Market Manipulation", "Priority of Transactions", "Additional Compensation"],
 "Standard V(B) requires members to separate fact from opinion in investment analysis. Presenting a forecast as a fact misleads the reader."),
("Where no regulation sets a period, CFA Institute recommends keeping records that support research and recommendations for at least:",
 "Seven years", ["Three years", "Five years", "Ten years"],
 "Standard V(C), Record Retention, recommends a minimum of seven years when local law and firm policy give no other requirement."),
("Which statement about the CFA designation is permitted?",
 "\"I passed all three levels\"", ["\"I am a CFA\"", "\"Charterholders earn higher returns\"", "\"I am a CFA Level II holder\""],
 "Under Standard VII(B), a member may state facts such as having passed all three levels. \"CFA\" is used as an adjective, never a noun, partial designations do not exist, and claims of superior performance are not allowed."),
("A trader buys and sells the same thinly traded stock among accounts he controls to make it look heavily traded. This breaches:",
 "Market Manipulation", ["Suitability", "Priority of Transactions", "Independence and Objectivity"],
 "Standard II(B) prohibits practices that distort prices or artificially inflate trading volume to mislead market participants."),
("A supervisor finds that a team member may have front-run client trades. Under Responsibilities of Supervisors she should first:",
 "Investigate and limit his activity", ["Wait for a formal complaint", "Report him to clients directly", "Ignore it if the trades were small"],
 "Standard IV(C) requires reasonable efforts to prevent and detect violations. On learning of a possible violation the supervisor should promptly investigate and restrict the employee's activities while that is done."),
("Under the Global Investment Performance Standards (GIPS), a claim of compliance can be made by:",
 "A firm, firm-wide", ["An individual portfolio manager", "One composite of a firm", "A performance software vendor"],
 "GIPS compliance is claimed on a firm-wide basis. Individuals and single products cannot claim compliance on their own, and vendors cannot claim it for software."),
("Under GIPS, a composite must include:",
 "Every fee-paying discretionary portfolio", ["Only the best-performing portfolios", "Only portfolios open for a full year", "Model portfolios as well as real ones"],
 "Composites must include every actual, fee-paying, discretionary portfolio managed to that strategy, so a firm cannot leave out poor performers."),
("An analyst who owns shares of a company writes a buy recommendation on it. Under Disclosure of Conflicts he should:",
 "Disclose the holding in the report", ["Sell the shares before writing it", "Stop covering the company", "Disclose it to his manager only"],
 "Standard VI(A) requires full, fair disclosure of matters that could impair independence, including ownership of the recommended security, to clients and the employer."),
("A manager uses client brokerage commissions to buy research. Under Loyalty, Prudence and Care, the research must:",
 "Help manage that client's account", ["Support the firm's marketing", "Serve only the firm's new clients", "Be disclosed to the issuer first"],
 "Client brokerage belongs to the client. Under Standard III(A), soft dollars may only buy products or services that help in managing that client's portfolio."),
("An analyst copies sections of another firm's report into her own without credit. This breaches:",
 "Misrepresentation", ["Loyalty to employer", "Knowledge of the Law", "Fair Dealing"],
 "Standard I(C) prohibits plagiarism. Factual data from recognized statistical sources may be used without credit, but another analyst's analysis and words may not."),
]
